{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793297698,"nonce":"YJfsSwdUHtgTSmU2lpefHEi8xC4=","title":"Yield and report progress within LSP import indexing","message":"Pocket Studio src/ui/TaskInspector.hx remained in the indexing state for a long time despite being a small component. An isolated release LSP reproduction took 56.668 seconds, with 360 typed files, 410 discovered import nodes, and roughly 39 seconds attributed to macros (nested stage timings overlap). The document eventually had zero diagnostics and correct Computed\u003cNull\u003cmodel.Task\u003e\u003e, Signal\u003cString\u003e, and generated-element hovers.\n\nThe existing document fibers have an 8 ms slice, but import compilation/retry boundaries and large macro reference graph scans do not all poll the scheduler. The UI only sees declarations before whole-graph typing, so it cannot identify the dependency currently being processed.\n\nAdd task-scoped import discovery/type/retry progress; yield at module boundaries, macro registry registration, and reference graph scans; avoid cloning discarded class bodies when constructing discovery headers. Verify a root blocked in an imported build macro still supplies partial declarations, a second page completes, and the original page reports ready after the macro is released.\n\nRelated broader scheduling issue: c7864710dd6916b7c3ed11f39f890a836f24a898fb3ac4e113a9f2f2d931c487. Total cold macro cost and shared typed dependency publication remain separate work.","files":null}]}